BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup|Jan 
 14 - MCP\, Agents and Skills Meetup|Online
DTSTART:20270114T110000
CATEGORIES:ai,data,devops,frontend,security,web
DESCRIPTION:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\
 nJoin our virtual meetup to hear talks from experts on cutting-edge topics
  across AI\, ML\, and computer vision.\n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\n
 Jan 14\, 2027\n9:00 AM - 11:00 AM PST\nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](
 https://voxel51.com/events/mcp-agents-skills-meetup-january-14-2027)**\n\n
 **From Demo to Production: Testing\, Scaling and Securing AI Agents with S
 pring AI**\n\nYour agent works perfectly in the demo. Then in production i
 t gives a wrong answer and nobody can tell why\, token costs spike\, it sl
 ows to a crawl under load\, and it calls a tool the user was never allowed
  to use. Building an agent is the easy part\; the real work is running it 
 like any other production service: tested\, observable\, scalable and secu
 re.\n\nSpring AI lets you do that with the Spring idioms and tooling you a
 lready know. This code-first session grows one example step by step. We st
 art with a simple ChatClient\, add advisors\, memory and RAG\, comparing t
 he options Spring AI offers for each\, and then let the agent act through 
 tool calling and MCP.\n\nThen come the production questions:\n\n* Testing:
  we catch regressions with LLM-as-judge evaluators and typed decision mode
 ls like Jev for fast\, cheap verdicts.\n* Observability: we add Micrometer
  traces\, logs and token usage across model and tool calls.\n* Scaling: we
  compare options for handling more concurrent users\, from self-hosting to
  a managed runtime such as Amazon Bedrock AgentCore.\n* Security: we use S
 pring Security to authenticate users and make sure only the MCP tools they
 're allowed to use ever get called. You'll leave with reusable Spring patt
 erns for agents that reason and act\, and the discipline to test\, observe
 \, scale and secure them in production.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Andrei 
 Shakirin](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrei-shakirin/) is a Solutions Arc
 hitect at Amazon Web Services and a former Pivotal Labs engineer\, special
 izing in the design of highly scalable systems and generative AI applicati
 ons. He is an active committer to several Spring and Apache open-source pr
 ojects\, contributor into MCP java SDK and a regular speaker at technical 
 conferences.\n\n**From Model Evals to System Evals: Evaluating Agents\, Ha
 rnesses\, and the Evaluators Themselves**\n\nEvery team shipping agents is
  quietly standing up two systems: the agent harness that plans\, retrieves
 \, calls tools\, and edits code — and the evaluation harness that decide
 s whether any of it can be trusted. Most teams only evaluate the first one
 's final output.\n\nThat is not enough. An agent can produce a correct ans
 wer through an unreliable\, wasteful\, or out-of-policy execution path\, a
 nd outcome-only checks will pass it. This talk argues for evaluating the w
 hole agent system: execution trajectories — tool correctness\, evidence 
 and grounding\, state handling\, policy compliance\, and efficiency — al
 ongside final outcomes\, using deterministic specification checks an agent
  cannot game\, model-based judges\, and human-calibrated benchmarks.\n\nWe
  separate capability evaluations from regression evaluations so harness or
  model changes can be gated in CI. Then we turn the lens around: the evalu
 ators themselves need evaluating — LLM judge calibration\, disagreement 
 analysis\, and where human review still belongs.\n\nAttendees leave with a
  production checklist for gating agent\, model\, and harness changes on sy
 stem-level evidence instead of final answers.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[D
 ipen Chawla](https://www.linkedin.com/in/dipen-chawla-bb4a49bb/) is an AI 
 engineer and researcher working on agentic AI systems and their evaluation
 \, including agent memory and clinical diagnostic reasoning work at Micros
 oft\, and data platform engineering at Walmart and S&P Global.\n\n**Leadin
 g an Army of AI Agents**\n\nOur AI coding agents now pick up tasks\, follo
 w team playbooks\, open pull requests and wait for review. This firsthand 
 engineering case study follows more than ten agents running in parallel an
 d shows how briefs\, shared context and verification loops keep their work
  reviewable.\nIt covers the failure modes too: confident wrong turns\, inc
 omplete verification\, review bottlenecks and the attention required from 
 human leaders.\nAttendees will learn a practical task-to-PR workflow\, how
  to make trust conditional on evidence\, and which measures expose the cos
 ts hidden by faster output. The focus is on agent workflow and operating d
 iscipline\, with concrete engineering examples rather than a product pitch
 .\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[John Adib](https://www.linkedin.com/in/MrAdib
 ) is a London-based engineering manager at Zapp\, a former CTO and two-tim
 e founder with 18+ years of software experience.\n\nAgents in Action: Putt
 ing AI Agents to Work on Visual AI Data\n\nWhat should an AI agent remembe
 r\, and what should it forget? In this talk\, we’ll explore the Voxel51 
 agent through the lens of memory\, examining how retaining the right conte
 xt and incorporating feedback can help agents improve over time. Through e
 xamples from visual AI workflows\, we’ll discuss what’s worth remember
 ing\, from dataset context and user preferences to corrections and success
 ful approaches\, and when to update or discard information that becomes ou
 tdated. We’ll also explore how feedback and evaluation can help us deter
 mine whether an agent is making better decisions\, offering practical insi
 ghts for building agents that become more useful with experience.\n\n*Abou
 t the Speaker*\n\n[Adonai Vera](https://www.linkedin.com/in/adonai-vera/) 
 is a Machine Learning Engineer and DevRel at Voxel51 with over 7 years of 
 experience building computer vision and machine learning models using Tens
 orFlow\, Docker\, and OpenCV.
LOCATION:Online event\,
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup|Jan 
 21 - AI\, ML and Computer Vision Meetup|Online
DTSTART:20270121T110000
CATEGORIES:ai,backend,cloud,data,devops,frontend,python
DESCRIPTION:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\
 nJoin our virtual meetup to hear talks from experts on cutting-edge topics
  across AI\, ML\, and computer vision.\n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\n
 Jan 21\, 2027\n9:00 AM - 11:00 AM PST\nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](
 https://voxel51.com/events/ai-ml-and-computer-vision-meetup-january-21-202
 7)**\n\n**Verifier-Gated Generative Tutoring: When the LLM Writes Lessons 
 but Never Decides Mastery**\n\nAdaptive tutors increasingly let LLMs both 
 generate content and decide mastery — a risky coupling. dino.study separ
 ates those roles: models draft interactive lessons under verifier gates\, 
 while deterministic Python BKT + FSRS tracks mastery and review timing. I'
 ll show the production architecture (Expo app + Bun API + Postgres prerequ
 isite graphs)\, cost (\\~$0.04/lesson)\, and an honest LLM-simulated N=30/
 arm dry-run that is not a human peer-reviewed study.\n\nThe talk is code-f
 orward with a live demo. Aimed at ML/CV practitioners curious about evalua
 tion\, mastery modeling\, and shipping educational AI.\n\n*About the Speak
 er*\n\n[Animesh Alang](https://www.linkedin.com/in/animesh-alang-232713132
 ) is a UCSC Computer Engineering junior building dino.study. He founded Sl
 ugAI\, serves as President of CruzHacks\, and was Cloud Track winner at NV
 IDIA Hack-a-Claw.\n\n**Building CAD for Machine Intelligence: The Future o
 f AI-Driven Engineering**\n\nWhat does it take to build AI systems that ca
 n reason about geometry\, design mechanical parts\, and automate engineeri
 ng workflows? In this talk\, we introduce Quartz\, a CAD system built for 
 machine intelligence\, and explore how high-performance geometry execution
 \, reinforcement learning\, and massively parallel environments enable AI 
 agents to learn engineering through design and iteration.\n\nWe demonstrat
 e how AI can create and modify complex assemblies while preserving editabl
 e CAD histories\, and share our vision for the future of AI-driven enginee
 ring.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Debao Huang](https://www.linkedin.com/in/
 debao-huang/) is currently a Research Scientist at Spectral Labs\, where h
 e develops AI-native CAD systems and foundation models for automating engi
 neering design. He obtained his Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineeri
 ng from The Ohio State University.\n\n**Does 70% Mean 70%? Building an Hon
 est Tennis Win-Probability Engine**\n\nWhen a model says a tennis player h
 as a 70% chance to win\, does that player win 70% of the time? I built Tie
 break\, a win-probability engine trained on about 900\,000 ATP matches tha
 t layers surface-adjusted Elo\, a point-by-point Markov chain and gradient
  boosting\, updating live as a match unfolds.\n\nEarly versions looked bet
 ter than they were because the same match leaked into both training and te
 st data\, and I show exactly how I caught it with a chronological\, match-
 level split. Reliability diagrams then show where the model is calibrated 
 and where it is overconfident.\n\nAttendees leave with a practical checkli
 st for building and honestly evaluating any ML model that outputs probabil
 ities.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Krrish Ghindani](https://www.linkedin.co
 m/in/krrishghindani/) is a Data Science junior at the University of San Fr
 ancisco on a full merit scholarship and leads USF's Data Science team at n
 ational competitions (1st place).\n\n**Can an Open-Weight Model Power a Ph
 ysical AI Agent? Serving Gemma 4 on Google TPUs with Voxel51**\n\nIn this 
 session\, we'll deploy Google's Gemma 4 on Cloud TPUs using vLLM-TorchTPU 
 and connect it to the Voxel51 Agent. Through a live demo and inference ben
 chmarks\, we'll explore how open-weight models can support Physical AI wor
 kflows\, examining latency\, throughput\, and the infrastructure tradeoffs
  behind building reliable AI agents.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Adonai Ver
 a](https://www.linkedin.com/in/adonai-vera/) is a Machine Learning Enginee
 r and DevRel at Voxel51 with over 7 years of experience building computer 
 vision and machine learning models using TensorFlow\, Docker\, and OpenCV.
LOCATION:Online event\,
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup|Feb 
 10 - Physical AI Meetup: Robot Brains and Models|Online
DTSTART:20270210T110000
CATEGORIES:ai,backend,cloud,data,devops,frontend,security
DESCRIPTION:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\
 nJoin us for the Physical AI\, ML and Computer Vision Meetup series focuse
 d on robotics use cases. In this session we will focus on robot brains and
  models.\n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\nFeb 10\, 2027\n9:00 AM - 11:00
  AM PST\nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](https://voxel51.com/events/phy
 sical-ai-ml-and-computer-vision-meetup-robot-brains-and-models-february-10
 -2027)**\n\n**Physical AI That Adapts**\n\nEvery vision model is trained o
 n a snapshot of the world\, but robots are deployed into one that keeps ch
 anging: lighting shifts\, friction drifts\, objects move\, actuators wear.
  Today the fix is a slow loop of collecting new data\, retraining\, valida
 ting and redeploying\, which can take weeks or months. In this talk\, we s
 how how Lazy Dynamics’ LazyInfer closes that gap by treating perception\
 , parameter learning and control as one Bayesian inference problem that ru
 ns on the device\, inside the control loop.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Bar
 t van Erp](https://www.linkedin.com/in/bart-van-erp/) is CTO at Lazy Dynam
 ics. He has a background in Electrical Engineering and a PhD in the field 
 of probabilistic machine learning.\n\n**Robotic World Models: Cloud Versus
  On-Edge**\n\nCloud-hosted robotic world models can draw on substantial co
 mpute\, while on-edge models promise local\, low-latency inference\; choos
 ing between them depends on the reliability of their predictions. We exami
 ne this question through a from-scratch\, gradient-verified reproduction o
 f the proprioceptive Robotic World Model for ANYmal D\, using recorded rob
 ot trajectories and CPU resources. Autoregressive training reproduces the 
 model's long-horizon advantage over teacher forcing\, while ensemble disag
 reement tracks the ordering of prediction errors but substantially underes
 timates their magnitude. On the released checkpoint's training episodes\, 
 disagreement is 33.4 times smaller than realized error at the method's 100
 -step rollout horizon\, making it a useful ranking signal but not a calibr
 ated error bar.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\nJoyjeet Singh is an independent 
 researcher in physics-informed machine learning and the sole author of thr
 ee arXiv preprints on implicit-reasoning models\, world-model reproducibil
 ity\, and the planning objectives used over learned latent dynamics.\n\n**
 Faster Physical AI Development with CoreWeave**\n\nRobot brains are gettin
 g bigger. Vision language action models\, world models and foundation poli
 cies all demand large training runs\, fast experimentation and serving tha
 t keeps up with real robots. In this session\, Narendra Kumar Vadapalli\, 
 Senior Specialist Field Engineer at CoreWeave\, walks through how physical
  AI teams are building the infrastructure behind these models today. Drawi
 ng on his work helping customers design and scale AI platforms on Kubernet
 es\, he will cover how teams organize multimodal training data\, run distr
 ibuted training and simulation side by side\, and move models from experim
 ent to deployment without rebuilding their stack each time. Attendees will
  leave with practical patterns for scaling robot learning workloads and a 
 look at what is coming next.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Narendra Kumar Vad
 apalli](https://www.linkedin.com/in/narenandu/) is a Senior Specialist Fie
 ld Engineer at CoreWeave\, where he has helped customers design and scale 
 AI infrastructure since 2024.\n\n**Robot Brains and Models with Voxel51**\
 n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Brent Griffin](https://www.linkedin.com/in/bren
 t-griffin-0b6325b5/) is a Principal Machine Learning Scientist at Voxel51\
 , specializing in low-cost learning on unstructured data at scale. He prev
 iously built and led the perception group at Agility Robotics and was an a
 ssistant research scientist at the University of Michigan\, working at the
  intersection of computer vision\, control\, and robot learning.
LOCATION:Online event\,
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup|Feb 
 25 - MCP\, Agents and Skills Meetup|Online
DTSTART:20270225T110000
CATEGORIES:ai,backend,cloud,data,devops,frontend,python,security
DESCRIPTION:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\
 nJoin our virtual meetup to hear talks from experts on cutting-edge topics
  across MCP\, agents and skills.\n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\nFeb 25
 \, 2027\n9:00 AM - 11:00 AM PST\nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](https:
 //voxel51.com/events/mcp-agents-skills-meetup-february-25-2027)**\n\n**You
 r Agent Crashed After a Tool Call. Now What?**\n\nAgent demos rarely show 
 what happens when the worker dies halfway through a tool workflow. I’ll 
 demonstrate crash recovery with LangGraph and Pydantic AI using Azure Dura
 ble Task Scheduler. I’ll show persisted progress\, retries\, and a human
  approval step. I’ll distinguish workflow recovery from external effects
 : preventing duplicate effects requires idempotency enforced by the extern
 al API.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Tamir Dresher](https://www.linkedin.com
 /in/tamirdresher/) is a Principal Engineer on Microsoft's Azure Serverless
  team. Tamir built the official Azure Durable Task Scheduler integrations 
 for LangGraph and Pydantic AI.\n\n**Before You Connect That MCP Server: Wh
 at It Can Reach\, What It Can Do**\n\nConnect an agent to an MCP server an
 d that server gets access to your credentials and a standing invitation to
  act for you. Independent researcher Syed Anas Mohiuddin is credited on 16
  GitHub security advisories for MCP servers\, covering server-side request
  forgery\, command injection\, missing authentication\, session hijack\, a
 nd credential leaks.\n\nThis talk goes through the checks to run before yo
 u connect a server: what it can reach on the network\, what it stores and 
 logs\, and which of its tools can change real systems. It then covers the 
 check that has to sit at run time\, between the agent and the action\, so 
 a poisoned tool description or a forged request meets a person before it r
 eaches production.\n\nAttendees get a short list of questions to put to an
 y MCP server they already run.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Michael Kantor](
 https://www.linkedin.com/in/kantorcodes) is President of HOL\, a nonprofit
  consortium that builds open standards for AI agent infrastructure. HOL's 
 product is HOL Guard\, a firewall for any AI agent\, which checks risky ac
 tions before they run. On 10 November 2026 he spoke online at [re]aligned\
 , a CanSecWest extension\, on testing runtime controls for agents: "Did th
 e Agent Guard Stop the Action\, or Only the Words?"\n\n**Flash Demo: Agent
 s in Action with Voxel51**\n\nIn this flash demo\, Adonai will show some o
 f the agent workflows the Voxel51 team has been building\, and how you can
  put agents to work on your own visual AI data with Voxel51. Expect a quic
 k\, hands-on look at what's possible today and how to try it yourself.\n\n
 *About the Speaker*\n\n[Adonai Vera](https://www.linkedin.com/in/adonai-ve
 ra/) is a Machine Learning Engineer and DevRel at Voxel51 with over 7 year
 s of experience building computer vision and machine learning models using
  TensorFlow\, Docker\, and OpenCV.
LOCATION:Online event\,
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup|Marc
 h 10 - Physical AI Meetup: Robotic Data Quality and Evaluation|Online
DTSTART:20270310T110000
CATEGORIES:ai,data,frontend
DESCRIPTION:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\
 nJoin us for the Physical AI\, ML and Computer Vision Meetup series focuse
 d on robotics use cases. In this session we will focus on robotic data qua
 lity and evaluation.\n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\nMar 10\, 2027\n9:0
 0 AM - 11:00 AM PST\nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](https://voxel51.co
 m/events/physical-ai-ml-and-computer-vision-meetup-robotic-data-quality-an
 d-evaluation-march-10-2027)**\n\n**Evaluating Robots in the Real World**\n
 \nDeploying robots is still one of the biggest gaps in robotics. A few han
 d-counted trials in the lab don't tell you how a robot will behave in the 
 real world. Kayoum will talk about why robot results are so hard to trust\
 , what data is worth capturing so a failure can be traced to its cause\, a
 nd how simulation and hardware testing can work together. Attendees will l
 eave with a practical way to set up evaluation that reflects real operatin
 g conditions and catches problems early.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Kayoum
  Djedidi](https://www.linkedin.com/in/kayoum-djedidi/) is CEO and co-found
 er of OORB\, a UC Berkeley SkyDeck startup building runfp\, the reliabilit
 y layer for robots in the field. A microelectronics and embedded systems e
 ngineer and PhD researcher in telecommunications at INRS Montréal\n\n**Wh
 en AI Meets the Robot Arm: What 500+ Real-World Tests Reveal About Robotic
  Intelligence**\n\nAI models are getting remarkably good at understanding 
 instructions\, reasoning through tasks\, and generating robotic actions. B
 ut how well do those capabilities translate into successful manipulation o
 n physical robots? At Mecka AI we evaluated a range of AI-driven robotic s
 ystems across more than 500 physical robot runs on laboratory manipulation
  tasks\, measuring not just whether systems can execute actions but whethe
 r they reliably complete the tasks they are given.\n\nThe results show a r
 eal gap between a model's ability to plan actions and a robot's ability to
  finish the job\, from missed grasps and object-handling errors to agents 
 that report success despite failing. This session covers how we designed t
 he evaluations\, measured task-level performance\, and analyzed failure pa
 tterns\, and what the findings mean for engineers choosing AI approaches f
 or real automation.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Harshavardhan Reddy Gajarla
 ](https://www.linkedin.com/in/harshavardhan-reddy18) is the VLA Engineerin
 g Lead at Mecka AI\, a Sequoia-backed robotics data company\, where he wor
 ks across the robot-learning stack and first-authored the WetLabs Benchmar
 k of frontier AI models on 500+ physical robot runs.\n\n**Faster Physical 
 AI Development with CoreWeave**\n\nPhysical AI teams generate more data th
 an they can ever label or review\, and the hard part is knowing which data
  and which experiments actually move a robot forward. In this session\, An
 u Vatsa\, Technical Leader for Robotics GTM at CoreWeave\, shares what he 
 has seen working with robotics and autonomous vehicle teams on their train
 ing and evaluation infrastructure. He will cover how teams track experimen
 ts and evaluations across huge multimodal datasets\, how they run simulati
 on and training at scale without stalling iteration\, and where the common
  bottlenecks appear as programs grow from a single robot to a fleet. Atten
 dees will come away with a practical picture of the infrastructure behind 
 fast\, reliable physical AI development and what is coming next to make it
  easier to go from data to deployed models.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Anu
  Vatsa](https://www.linkedin.com/in/anushrav-vatsa/) is Technical Leader f
 or Robotics GTM at CoreWeave\, where he helps teams build and scale physic
 al AI. He joined CoreWeave through its acquisition of Weights & Biases\, w
 here he was an AI Solutions Engineer working with robotics and autonomous 
 vehicle teams. Before that he led solutions engineering at Fiddler AI\, fo
 cusing on explainable AI.\n\n**Robotic Data Quality and Evaluation with Vo
